4.Reference：HKDSE Math M2 2012 Q5
$$
\begin{aligned}
& y=x+2 \sin x \\
& \frac{d y}{d x}=1+2 \cos x
\end{aligned}
$$
For $\frac{d y}{d x}=0$ ,
$$
\begin{aligned}
1+2 \cos x & =0 \\
\cos x & =\frac{-1}{2} \\
x & =\frac{2 \pi}{3} \text { or } \frac{4 \pi}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline$x$ & $0<x<\frac{2 \pi}{3}$ & $x=\frac{2 \pi}{3}$ & $\frac{2 \pi}{3}<x<\frac{4 \pi}{3}$ & $x=\frac{4 \pi}{3}$ & $\frac{4 \pi}{3}<x<2 \pi$ \\
\hline$\frac{d y}{d x}$ & + & 0 & - & 0 & + \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { When } x=\frac{2 \pi}{3}, y=\frac{2 \pi}{3}+2 \sin \frac{2 \pi}{3}=\frac{2 \pi}{3}+\sqrt{3} \\
& \therefore \quad\left(\frac{2 \pi}{3}, \frac{2 \pi}{3}+\sqrt{3}\right) \text { is a maximum point. }
\end{aligned}
$$
1M（for testing）
1A
（3）

（a）
Construct rectangles QMSR and PNST as shown in the figure.
In $\triangle S R O$ and $\triangle S T O$ ,
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
& \angle S R O=\angle S T O=90^{\circ} \quad(\text { given }) \\
& R S=T S=\sqrt{3} \mathrm{~m} \quad(\text { given }) \\
& O S=O S \\
\therefore & \triangle S R O \cong \triangle S T O \quad(\text { common side }) \\
\therefore & O R=O T=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{\tan \frac{\pi}{3}} \mathrm{~m}=1 \mathrm{~m}
\end{array}
$$

Consider $\triangle M N S$ .
$$
\begin{aligned}
& N S=(x-1) \mathrm{m} \\
& M S=(y-1) \mathrm{m}
\end{aligned}
$$
By the cosine formula,
$$
\begin{align*}
(x-1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}-2(x-1)(y-1) \cos \frac{2 \pi}{3} & =14^{2}  \tag{1M}\\
x^{2}-2 x+1+y^{2}-2 y+1+x y-x-y+1 & =196 \\
x^{2}+y^{2}-3 x-3 y+x y & =193
\end{align*}
$$
Differentiating（1）with respect to $x$ ,we have
$$
\begin{align*}
2 x+2 y \frac{d y}{d x}-3-3 \frac{d y}{d x}+x \frac{d y}{d x}+y & =0  \tag{1M}\\
\frac{d y}{d x} & =-\frac{2 x+y-3}{x+2 y-3} \tag{1}
\end{align*}
$$
（b）When $x=11$ ,
$$
\begin{align*}
11^{2}+y^{2}-3(11)-3 y+11 y & =193  \tag{1M}\\
y^{2}+8 y-105 & =0 \\
(y-7)(y+15) & =0
\end{align*}
$$
$$
y=7 \text { or }-15 \text { (rejected) }
$$
1A
By the sine formula,we have
$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{y-1}{\sin \theta} & =\frac{14}{\sin \frac{2 \pi}{3}} \\
y & =\frac{28}{\sqrt{3}} \sin \theta+1 \tag{2}
\end{align*}
$$
When $y=7, \sin \theta=\frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{14}$ and
$$
\begin{align*}
\cos \theta & =\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{14}\right)^{2}}=\frac{13}{14}  \tag{1A}\\
\frac{d y}{d t} & =\frac{d y}{d x} \times \frac{d x}{d t} \\
& =-\frac{2 x+y-3}{x+2 y-3} \times(-2) \tag{1M}
\end{align*}
$$
When $x=11$ and $y=7$ ,
$$
\begin{align*}
\frac{d y}{d t} & =-\frac{2(11)+7-3}{11+2(7)-3} \times(-2) \\
& =\frac{26}{11} \tag{1A}
\end{align*}
$$

Differentiating（2）with respect to $t$ ,we have
$$
\begin{equation*}
\frac{d y}{d t}=\frac{28}{\sqrt{3}} \cos \theta \frac{d \theta}{d t} \tag{1M}
\end{equation*}
$$
When $y=7$ ,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d y}{d t}=\frac{28}{\sqrt{3}} & \times \frac{13}{14} \times \frac{d \theta}{d t}=\frac{26}{\sqrt{3}} \times \frac{d \theta}{d t} \\
\therefore \quad \frac{26}{\sqrt{3}} \times \frac{d \theta}{d t} & =\frac{26}{11} \\
\frac{d \theta}{d t} & =\frac{\sqrt{3}}{11}
\end{aligned}
$$
∴ The rate of increase of $\theta$ is $\frac{\sqrt{3}}{11}$ radian per second.

6.Reference：HKDSE Math M2 2021 Q10
(a) $P Q=\sqrt{(p-10)^{2}+9}+\sqrt{p^{2}+4}$ 1M
$$
\frac{d P Q}{d p}=\frac{p-10}{\sqrt{(p-10)^{2}+9}}+\frac{p}{\sqrt{p^{2}+4}}
$$
1M
For $\frac{d P Q}{d p}=0$ ,we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{p-10}{\sqrt{(p-10)^{2}+9}} & =-\frac{p}{\sqrt{p^{2}+4}} \\
\frac{(p-10)^{2}}{(p-10)^{2}+9} & =\frac{p^{2}}{p^{2}+4} \\
p^{2}(p-10)^{2}+4(p-10)^{2} & =p^{2}(p-10)^{2}+9 p^{2} \\
4(p-10)^{2}-9 p^{2} & =0 \\
(5 p-20)(-p-20) & =0
\end{aligned}
$$
$$
\begin{equation*}
p=4 \quad \text { or } \quad p=-20(\text { rejected }) \tag{1 M}
\end{equation*}
$$
$$
\begin{equation*}
\therefore \quad a=4 \tag{1A}
\end{equation*}
$$
(b) （i）Let $A$ square units be the area of $\triangle O P Q$ .
$$
\begin{align*}
& A=\frac{1}{2} p\left[\sqrt{(p-10)^{2}+9}+\sqrt{p^{2}+4}\right]  \tag{1 M}\\
& \frac{d A}{d p}=\frac{1}{2} p\left[\frac{p-10}{\sqrt{(p-10)^{2}+9}}+\frac{p}{\sqrt{p^{2}+4}}\right]+\frac{1}{2}\left[\sqrt{(p-10)^{2}+9}+\sqrt{p^{2}+4}\right]  \tag{1 M}\\
& \left.\therefore \quad \frac{d A}{d p}\right|_{p=4}=2\left(\frac{-6}{\sqrt{45}}+\frac{4}{\sqrt{20}}\right)+\frac{1}{2}(\sqrt{45}+\sqrt{20})=\frac{5 \sqrt{5}}{2} \neq 0 . \tag{1 M}
\end{align*}
$$
Hence,$A$ does not attain its minimum value when $p=4$ .
Thus,the claim is disagreed.
(ii) Since $O P=\sqrt{p^{2}+\left(-\sqrt{p^{2}+4}\right)^{2}}$ ,we have $O P=\sqrt{2 p^{2}+4}$ .
At time $t$ seconds,we have $\frac{d O P}{d t}=\frac{2 p}{\sqrt{2 p^{2}+4}}\left(\frac{d p}{d t}\right)$ .
As $2=\frac{2(4)}{\sqrt{2\left(4^{2}\right)+4}}\left(\left.\frac{d p}{d t}\right|_{p=4}\right)$ ,we have $\left.\frac{d p}{d t}\right|_{p=4}=\frac{3}{2}$ .
Let $w$ units be the perimeter of $\triangle O P Q$ .
$$
\begin{align*}
& w=O P+O Q+P Q=O P+\sqrt{p^{2}+(p-10)^{2}+9}+P Q  \tag{1 M}\\
& \frac{d w}{d t}=\frac{d O P}{d t}+\left[\frac{p+(p-10)}{\sqrt{p^{2}+(p-10)^{2}+9}}+\frac{d P Q}{d p}\right]\left(\frac{d p}{d t}\right)  \tag{1M}\\
& \left.\therefore \quad \frac{d w}{d t}\right|_{p=4}=2+\left(\frac{-2}{\sqrt{61}}+0\right)\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)=2-\frac{3 \sqrt{61}}{61}
\end{align*}
$$
Thus,the required rate of change is $\left(2-\frac{3 \sqrt{61}}{61}\right)$ units per second.

